Your universal response formula

Memorize this:

“I agree with [X]. But that establishes [X], not necessarily platform liability. What specifically about this situation makes the platform legally liable?”

That’s your emergency weapon.

You can adapt it to almost anything.

Group 5: “People have died because of misinformation.”

“I agree the harm can be very serious. But that establishes the harm. What specifically makes the platform legally liable for the user’s statement?”

“They profit from misinformation.”

“That’s an important distinction. If the problem is the platform’s own conduct or amplification, let’s focus liability on that conduct. Why does that require liability for the user’s statement itself?”

“They knew it was false.”

“Then knowledge becomes your trigger. What exactly must the platform know, and who determines that the claim is false?”

“They should remove it once reported.”

“Then the report appears to trigger the duty. Does receiving a report make something false, or does the platform first have to determine whether the report is correct?”

“AI can detect misinformation now.”

“That may improve moderation. But detection capability doesn’t answer the legal question. What error rate is acceptable when getting it wrong creates legal liability?”

“Victims deserve compensation.”

“Agreed. Victims deserve remedies. But what specific wrong did the platform commit that makes it the legally liable party?”

“Europe regulates platforms.”

“Then let’s look at the specific duty being imposed. Is the liability based on the user’s false statement, or on the platform violating a defined legal duty?”
